WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:18.240
This is the lab, basically,
where we do all of our,

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.463
all of our mixing and potion-ing.

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:23.670
So as you can probably
tell, we've mixed a few

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:28.720
batches of blood on this counter,
but I can take you through

00:00:29.601 --> 00:00:31.860
a batch if you like.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.280
Most of your run-of-the-mill bloods

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:38.350
start off with corn syrup,
you know, it gives it a little

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:41.630
viscosity, if it was going to
be a regular everyday blood,

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:46.320
we'd put some soap in it,
because that helps it clean up.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:47.153
Interviewer: Oh really?

00:00:47.153 --> 00:00:48.770
Pam: Like when you, when
you get it in your wardrobe

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:51.910
or something, it helps it wash out.

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:55.883
If you were to just put
color in here right now,

00:00:57.190 --> 00:01:00.050
it would kinda come out
looking like cherry cough syrup

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:03.010
because blood is a little
opaque, it's not completely

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:07.893
see-through, so we add an opacifier to it,

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:13.470
to make it opaque before
we start to color it.

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:18.430
So, basically, what you get
is kind of this white-looking

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:21.994
slurry, then we can start to color it.

00:01:21.994 --> 00:01:24.220
And we go through a lot of food coloring.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:26.080
Obviously, the base is red.

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:30.750
Now, you're never really going
to get to a really realistic

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:33.080
blood red with just red food color,

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:36.690
so what we do is we add a little blue,

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:40.690
or black, and then that
gives us a little depth.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:43.383
So now you know, it looks more like

00:01:43.383 --> 00:01:46.749
your classic, classic blood.

00:01:46.749 --> 00:01:51.740
Now, we can take this, and take
it out here and do a little

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:55.219
something with it if you like.

00:01:55.219 --> 00:01:56.052
Interviewer: Yeah, sure.

00:01:56.052 --> 00:02:01.052
Pam: So we can do the, maybe
a blood spray, and maybe the

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:03.810
you know, classic blow out the
back of your head on the wall

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:04.765
kind of thing, if you want?

00:02:04.765 --> 00:02:05.598
(Laughs)

00:02:05.598 --> 00:02:06.431
Interviewer: Yeah, sure!

00:02:06.431 --> 00:02:08.540
Pam: For this, sometimes,
you'd hide them behind

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:12.130
somebody, and then, you know,
at the moment where they

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:15.203
get shot in the head, then
splat against the wall.

00:02:16.684 --> 00:02:18.351
Three, two, one...

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:25.930
Now if we do more pressure,

00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:27.699
then it's a little more violent.

00:02:27.699 --> 00:02:29.282
Interviewer: Right.

00:02:30.094 --> 00:02:31.087
Okay yeah go for it.

00:02:31.087 --> 00:02:33.726
Okay, three, two, one.

00:02:33.726 --> 00:02:35.010
(laughs)

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:35.843
No!

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:41.576
Wow, that is brutal.

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:54.963
So this is going to be more directed.

00:03:03.793 --> 00:03:05.713
Interviewer: Okay.

00:03:05.713 --> 00:03:08.123
Pam: That's for when you
get sliced in the neck and--

00:03:08.123 --> 00:03:09.485
(laughs)

00:03:09.485 --> 00:03:12.530
it sprays, (laughs) it sprays out with--

00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:14.363
Interviewer: Right.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.680
Pam: So if you're doing
blood on a floor that you

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:31.540
can't really get blood on
because it's gonna stain,

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:33.650
or on carpet or you know stuff like that,

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:37.530
then we make these rubber ones
that we can just throw down

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:39.863
on the floor and then pick up and go away.

